00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.839
So here’s the question: let’s say you
hop on a plane from New York to Amsterdam

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:17.240
then transfer onto a plane to Cape Town, South
Africa.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.710
Upon arrival in Amsterdam, passengers just
pop out into the terminal and then reboard

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:22.710
the next plane.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:27.050
You never would have gone through passport
control, so which country were you in during

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.070
your connection?

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:32.469
The simple answer is the Netherlands, but
you weren’t in the same Netherlands as people

00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:34.170
in downtown Amsterdam.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.019
The international zone of airports is part
of the country in which it is physically located,

00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:40.090
more or less.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.380
It almost operates as a separate country because
a border control agent would say you weren’t

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.390
in the Netherlands but a police officer would
say you were.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.749
You were in the Netherlands in a legal sense
but not in an immigration sense.

00:00:51.749 --> 00:00:53.729
This can create complications.

00:00:53.729 --> 00:00:58.389
Chinese citizens, for example, don’t need
a visa to transfer from one flight to another

00:00:58.389 --> 00:01:02.280
through Amsterdam Schiphol Airport but they
do need a visa to enter the Netherlands.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.960
If a Chinese citizen without a visa commits
a crime in Amsterdam Airport that crime must

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.170
be tried Dutch court, but if they don’t
have a visa the Chinese offender can’t legally

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.640
enter the Netherlands.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.700
The country therefore has the rather amazing
ability to create a floating international

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:24.780
zone around the Chinese Citizen—no matter
where they go, they are not in the Netherlands

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.540
even if they are walking down the streets
of Amsterdam.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.670
They will never have gone through passport
control or had their passport stamped but

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.300
they’ll still be in the Netherlands physically.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.000
Of course, though, they’ll always be in
the captivity of police officers.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.060
The question of what country you’re in in
airports can get a little more complicated

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.670
thanks to the USA.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.660
The US operates a unique system of customs
pre-clearance facilities at a number of airports

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.670
and some train stations and ports around the
world.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:57.050
Essentially, when flying from Dublin to New
York, for example, you clear US customs in

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:01.390
Dublin so that once you arrive in New York
you can immediately walk strait out of the

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.400
airport with no further checks just like a
domestic traveller.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:11.280
So, once you pass through US customs in Ireland
you’re in the US in an immigration-sense,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.290
but here’s where it gets tricky.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.890
You’re in the US, but you’re not on US
soil.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:21.530
Irish laws still apply past US customs in
Dublin airport.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.480
Of course, there are exceptions.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.590
The US border agents working the facility
are, on the other hand, subject to US laws

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.120
despite being in Ireland.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.200
They are essentially treated like diplomats.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.660
Crime is pretty rare in airports so this doesn’t
normally cause problems, but consider this.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:42.530
There’s a pre-clearance facility in Abu
Dhabi Airport in the United Arab Emirates….

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:48.280
where homosexuality is illegal and can be
punished with fines, prison time, or worse.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.610
That means that if you got a little frisky
past US customs in Abu Dhabi Airport, you

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.770
could technically be arrested while in the
US for being gay.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.940
Of course this would never happen because
it would create a diplomatic incident of monumental

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:04.310
proportions, but the point is that it could
happen.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:09.310
Speaking of diplomacy, more jurisdictional
fuzziness comes with the United Nations headquarters

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:10.830
in New York.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:15.990
The territory this building is on is technically
international territory—not part of the

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:16.990
US.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:21.840
In general US laws apply, but the UN can,
at their own discretion make their own laws

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.970
that trump US law.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.780
For example, the UN headquarters in New York
is allowed to issue stamps and run their own

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:33.570
postal service for its employees—something
that would be illegal in the US.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:37.830
There are more ways to visit countries without
going through immigration.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:42.380
Back in the 40’s, Russia and Estonia re-drew
their border and ended up making a bit of

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:47.440
a peninsula of Russia jutting out into Estonia
between the towns of Lutepää and Sesniki.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.050
As a part of the Soviet Union, it wasn’t
a problem for Estonia to build the road between

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:56.599
the two villages through the peninsula since
the borders were rather porous.

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:01.650
When the Soviet Union fell, though, the border
became one of the most guarded in the world

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.390
and therefore Sesniki was cut off from the
rest of Estonia.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:10.150
So, Russia decided to allow cars to drive
the short route with no border controls, no

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.760
paperwork, nothing as long as they don’t
stop.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.299
Border agents watch cameras at both ends to
make sure that any car that goes in one end

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:22.810
comes out the other and sends in agents if
a car takes too long, but this road allows

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:27.400
individuals to visit one of the most guarded
countries on earth without a visa or even

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.080
a passport.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.620
Despite the US’ affinity for large walls,
secure borders, and heavily armed agents,

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:39.480
you can legally visit the US without passing
through border controls or immigration.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.210
As the longest international border in the
world, there are actually quite a few unguarded

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:48.810
border crossings on the US-Canada border,
but when crossing one you are still required

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:53.420
to self-report to the nearest border patrol
station, with one exception.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:58.580
Hyder, Alaska has the unique distinction of
being the only town in the southern half of

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:02.570
Alaska’s panhandle to be connected to the
outside world by road.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.970
That is, though, because it in directly across
the border from Canada.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:12.380
Given that, the only roads into Hyder come
from Canada and you can’t drive anywhere

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.280
in the United States from Hyder.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.020
There are therefore no US border controls
when passing into Hyder and there’s no requirement

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.280
to self-report to a border patrol station.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:27.520
There is, however, a Canadian border control
station when passing back into Canada from

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:33.229
Hyder meaning you could accidentally cross
into the US without a passport and get stuck.

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:37.290
Hyder is essentially a Canadian town stuck
in Alaska.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:41.950
The residents unofficially use the timezone
of British Columbia rather than Alaska, use

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:47.330
Canadian dollars, have Canadian phone numbers,
and use Canadian fire and ambulance services.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:51.889
Perhaps most interesting, however, is that
you need a passport to travel to the United

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:53.729
States from Hyder.

00:05:53.729 --> 00:05:57.919
While it doesn't have an airport, it does
have a seaplane base with regularly scheduled

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:01.030
commercial flights to Ketchikan, Alaska.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:07.320
Even though this flight is fully within the
same country and state, upon arrival in Ketchikan

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.509
all passengers are sent through US customs.

00:06:10.509 --> 00:06:15.180
It makes sense—the reason Hyder doesn’t
have US customs in is because there’s no

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.960
way to get out to the rest of the US—but
this flight is the only time that you need

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.760
a passport to travel from one part of the
US to another.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.420
Now for the grandaddy of “which country
am I in,” questions.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.449
Which country are you in while in a plane.

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:35.760
Let’s say you’re flying in an Irish registered
airplane from Paris over Canada to New York.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.270
Are you in Ireland, France, Canada, or the
United States?

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.430
This gets super complicated.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:45.650
According to immigration law, you’re not
in any country, just like in an International

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:50.600
Airport even though you are physically in
the sovereign territory of Canada.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:55.229
Each country sets its own upper limit to its
sovereignty above earth, but all countries

00:06:55.229 --> 00:06:58.460
have their limit above the altitude at which
planes fly.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:03.550
From a legal view, however, within that plane
you could be in Ireland, France, Canada, and

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.759
the United States all at once.

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:08.850
Jurisdiction for crimes committed in the air
gets really fuzzy.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:13.710
Depending on their own laws, each country
could prosecute the same crime.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:18.590
Most of the time the laws that apply are those
of the aircraft’s country of registration—Ireland

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:24.030
in this case—but assuming the plane continued
to its final destination in the US, the arresting

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:28.890
officers for a crime would be US police and
therefore, unless another country claimed

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:34.350
jurisdiction, the trial would take plane in
the US under US law since the plane landed

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:35.710
in the US.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.449
However, Canada could also claim jurisdiction.

00:07:38.449 --> 00:07:44.770
In 1988 Pan Am flight 103 was brought down
by terrorists over Lockerbie, Scotland.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:50.110
Of course jurisdiction was a huge issue especially
considering the suspects were Libyan, but

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:54.419
in the end the trial was held in a Scottish
court under Scot law.

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:58.960
Since it was overflying Scotland and therefore
in Scottish territory when the plane was brought

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:04.340
down, they got jurisdiction, however, for
complicated diplomatic reasons relating the

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:09.470
extradition of the suspects, the trial was
actually held in an old US Air Force Base

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:13.680
in the Netherlands that was temporarily ceded
over to Scotland.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.220
The rather disappointing answer to which country
you’re in when flying is that it depends.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.710
Countries will just claim or ignore their
jurisdiction depending on convenience.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:33.080
In the end, the only way to find out might
just be to hop on a flight and commit a crime.

